    <region left="18" top="8073" width="760" height="26" color="#000000" fontSize="12">
      <text lang="eng" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
        <content>
          <p style="font-size: 12px; font-weight: bold;">Q/A</p>
        </content>
      </text>
    </region>
    <region left="18" top="8107" width="760" height="146" color="#000000" fontSize="10">
      <text lang="eng" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
        <content>
          <p>Why the discharge head differs from the zenith head: between the pressure gauge at the pump discharge and the top of the pipe, the water loses energy to friction along the vertical pipe and to the minor losses in the tee, rotameter, ball valve, elbows, and the "Y" fitting at the top. The discharge head is the energy the pump delivers, while the zenith head is what is left after those losses, so the discharge head should be the larger of the two. The difference also grows with height because a taller pipe has more length for friction. In our data the zenith head is about equal to the discharge head for pipe 5 and larger than it for pipe 6, which is not physically possible, so part of the difference there is measurement error, for example the gauge not sitting at the same height as the discharge port, the zenith height being measured to the wrong reference point, or the gauge tare drifting.</p>
        </content>
      </text>
    </region>
    <region left="18" top="8261" width="760" height="146" color="#000000" fontSize="10">
      <text lang="eng" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
        <content>
          <p>Factors that contribute to pump efficiency: (1) hydraulic losses - friction and turbulence as the water flows through the impeller and casing, plus shock losses when the flow enters the blades at the wrong angle; (2) volumetric (leakage) losses - water that recirculates through the clearance between the impeller and casing instead of leaving the discharge; (3) mechanical losses - bearing, seal, and disk friction on the spinning impeller; and (4) the electric motor and drive losses, since we measured electrical power rather than shaft power. Impeller design, running clearances, rotational speed, fluid viscosity, and pump size all affect these losses. A magnetic drive pump also loses energy in the magnetic coupling. (Reference: Cengel and Cimbala, Fluid Mechanics: Fundamentals and Applications, Chapter 14, Turbomachinery.)</p>
        </content>
      </text>
    </region>
    <region left="18" top="8415" width="760" height="146" color="#000000" fontSize="10">
      <text lang="eng" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
        <content>
          <p>Why the efficiency changes with flowrate: efficiency is the useful power ρgQh divided by the input power. At zero flow (valve closed) the pump still draws power but delivers no flow, so the efficiency is zero. As the flow increases the useful power rises faster than the losses, so the efficiency rises to a maximum at the best efficiency point (BEP), where the flow enters the impeller blades at their design angle. Beyond the BEP the head falls off and the friction and shock losses grow with the square of the velocity, so the efficiency drops again. For the centrifugal testbench the input power was the same at every setting (237.9 V × 4.1 A), so its efficiency followed Q·ΔP and was highest at the largest flowrate we could reach. The magnetic pump efficiency peaked at about 5.6% near 1.05 to 1.15 GPM (pipes 4 and 5) and fell off at both higher and lower flowrates, which shows its best efficiency point.</p>
        </content>
      </text>
    </region>
    <region left="18" top="8569" width="760" height="78" color="#000000" fontSize="10">
      <text lang="eng" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
        <content>
          <p>Magnetic drive pump vs. the Lab 6 system: yes, the magnetic pump curve crosses the system curve, so the pump could push flow through the Lab 6 piping. The operating point is at about 1.24 GPM with a head of about 2.09 m. This is at the very low-flow end of the system curve, well below the 3.5 GPM maximum we measured in Lab 6, because the magnetic pump can only produce about 2.7 m of head.</p>
        </content>
      </text>
    </region>
    <region left="18" top="8655" width="760" height="78" color="#000000" fontSize="10">
      <text lang="eng" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
        <content>
          <p>Centrifugal pump vs. the Lab 6 system: yes, the centrifugal pump curve crosses the system curve, so it could push flow through the Lab 6 piping. The operating point is at about 2.43 GPM with a head of about 11.5 m. Because we have no centrifugal readings between 0 and 8 GPM, this point uses a straight line between the 0% and 20% settings, so it is an estimate.</p>
        </content>
      </text>
    </region>
  </regions>
</worksheet>
